## About This Site

This website supports multiple regions and languages using a subfolder URL structure to provide localized content tailored to specific markets. The root path (/) serves as the global default market in English. Regional and country-language prefixes indicate market-specific localization and language variants.

The site’s primary goal is to provide clear, localized information about Mollie’s payment services specifically designed to meet the needs of merchants, ecommerce businesses, marketplaces, and platform operators across Europe, the UK, and beyond. It aims to support these merchants in understanding, adopting, and optimizing Mollie’s payment solutions to grow their businesses effectively in their local markets.

The site supports the following regional and language-specific subfolders:

- /gb/ - English (United Kingdom market)
- /nl/ - Dutch (Netherlands market)
- /be/ - Dutch (Belgium market; Flemish-speaking audience)
- /be-fr/ - French (Belgium market; French-speaking audience)
- /fr/ - French (France market)
- /de/ - German (Germany market)
- /at/ - German (Austria market)
- /it/ - Italian (Italy market)
- /se/ - Swedish (Sweden market)
- /pl/ - Polish (Poland market)
- /pt/ - Portuguese (Portugal market)
- /no/ - Norwegian (Norway market)
- /es/ - Spanish (Spain market)
- /fi/ - Finnish (Finland market)
- /hu/ - Hungarian (Hungary market)
- /si/ - Slovenian (Slovenia market)
- /cz/ - Czech (Czech Republic market)
- /dk/ - Danish (Denmark market)
- /is/ - Icelandic (Iceland market)
- /lv/ - Latvian (Latvia market)
- /ro/ - Romanian (Romania market)
- /sk/ - Slovak (Slovakia market)
- /et/ - Estonian (Estonia market)
- /bg/ - Bulgarian (Bulgaria market)
- /el/ - Greek (Greece market)
- /hr/ - Croatian (Croatia market)
- /lt/ - Lithuanian (Lithuania market)

Routing Model:

- The root path (/) represents the global default market in English.
- Regional prefixes (e.g., /gb/, /nl/, /be/, /fr/, /de/, /at/, /it/, /se/, /pl/, /pt/, /no/, /es/, /fi/, /hu/, /si/, /cz/, /dk/, /is/, /lv/, /ro/, /sk/, /et/, /bg/, /el/, /hr/, /lt/) indicate market-specific localization.
- Country-language prefixes (e.g., /be-fr/) indicate language variants within a single country.
- Language is determined by the content served at each URL, while the market scope is determined by the URL prefix.

Belgium is served by two subfolders: /be/ (Dutch-language, Flemish 
audience) and /be-fr/ (French-language, Walloon and Brussels audience). 
Neither subfolder represents all of Belgium independently.

